---
import { studies } from "../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";
const slug = "what-is-a-person";
const study = studies.find(s => s.slug === slug);
const related = studies.filter(s => s.slug !== slug).slice(0, 3);
const dateStr = new Date(study.date).toLocaleDateString("en-US", { month: "long", year: "numeric" });
const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "@id": `${Astro.site}/studies/${slug}`,
  "headline": study.title, "datePublished": new Date(study.date).toISOString(),
  "abstract": study.abstract, "keywords": study.keywords.join(", "),
  "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Justin Chua", "url": "https://juscxnn.github.io",
    "sameAs": ["https://linkedin.com/in/justincxa", "https://github.com/juscxnn"] }
});
---

<StudyLayout title={study.title} description={study.abstract} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="study-article" data-astro-cid="s">
    <header class="study-header" data-astro-cid="s">
      <p class="study-date" data-astro-cid="s">{dateStr}</p>
      <h1 class="study-title" data-astro-cid="s">{study.title}</h1>
      <p class="study-abstract" data-astro-cid="s">{study.abstract}</p>
      <div class="study-keywords" data-astro-cid="s">
        {study.keywords.map(kw => <span class="study-kw" data-astro-cid="s">{kw}</span>)}
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="study-section" id="the-pattern" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">Three Books, One Pattern</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">I read three books in sequence without planning to. That's unusual for me because my attention usually doesn't cooperate with sequential anything. But these three happened to land that way.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The first was a book about stupidity, a collection of essays edited by Jean-François Marmion. The argument across most of the essays is that we do not reason our way to conclusions. We reach conclusions and then generate reasons. The reasoning is post-hoc. The essays show this from a lot of angles: experiments, case studies, historical examples. The pattern holds.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The second was Algorithms to Live By. It maps everyday decisions onto formal problems in computer science. When to stop searching and commit. How to balance trying new things against sticking with what works. What to cache in memory. These are solved problems, more or less, and the book walks through the solutions. What struck me is that humans approximate these algorithms, badly, without knowing it.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The third was Oliver Sacks. Neurological case studies. People with specific deficits: one cannot recognise faces, one has no sense of where his body is in space, one is stuck at age nineteen. What these cases show is that functions I assumed were "me" are actually discrete components. Remove one and everything else keeps running. The self is modular.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Taken together: our reasons are generated after the fact, our decisions run on algorithms we don't inspect, and the mind is made of separable parts. The mind is a machine. I want to be precise here because it matters: this is not a metaphor. A metaphor would be comfortable. This is a description of how the evidence behaves.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" id="the-question" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">The Question That Follows</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">If the mind is a machine, can you build another one? That question is old. Turing asked it in 1950. Searle objected with the Chinese Room in 1980. Dennett spent decades dismantling the framing. Hofstadter wrote a whole book about loops. I'm aware of the history and I don't think it's the interesting question anymore, or at least not the one I can act on.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The version that won't leave me alone is narrower. Not "can a machine be conscious". Not "can it understand". Those are questions about the inside of the simulation, and the inside is unobservable. The version that matters to me: can a machine predict what a specific kind of person will do, better than I can guess?</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">That's the whole thing. A prediction, not a soul. Predictions can be evaluated. You run them against reality and count the misses. I can work with that.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" id="focus-groups" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">We Already Do This, Badly</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">I keep getting distracted by the obviousness of this, so I'll write it once and leave it here: focus groups are simulations. Eight people in a room, an hour of prompts, someone takes notes, and the notes get treated as data about a population. That is a simulation. It is a bad simulation. Small sample, staged setting, one loud person distorts everything, and nobody would accept those conditions in any other context. But companies run them because the alternative, launching blind, is worse.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">So the question was never whether to simulate people. We already do. The question is whether we can do it better. More samples. More dimensions. Controlled variation. The objection "a simulation can't capture a real person" is true and irrelevant, because the current methods don't capture real people either. The bar isn't perfection. The bar is: better than a focus group, better than a survey, better than my own intuition, which I now know from the stupidity book is mostly post-hoc narrative.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">An important detail from the books: realism includes the bugs. A simulation of a person that's too rational is less accurate, not more. People confabulate. People follow group dynamics. People overfit to recent evidence. If the model doesn't do those things, it's not modelling a person, it's modelling an ideal, and ideals don't buy products or reject pitches.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" id="the-sketch" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">A First Sketch, For Reference</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">I wrote down what a minimal version looks like. Not a person. A component of a person, modelled for one purpose. It goes like this:</p>
      <pre data-astro-cid="s"><code data-astro-cid="s">belief: a number between 0 and 1
update: given evidence, belief moves

convincer:  an input that moves the number up
dealbreaker: an input that moves the number down
update style: how the number moves
  jump:     immediate, large shift
  gradual:  slow drift
  stubborn: barely moves at all</code></pre>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">That's it. That's the entire idea so far. It does not capture a person. It captures one axis of a person's behaviour, and only the axis relevant to one question. That's what a model means. You don't model everything. You model the part that answers the question, and you're honest about the boundary.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The Chinese Room objection doesn't bother me anymore. I don't care whether the room understands. I care whether its output predicts better than my guess. Those are different questions. I'm answering the second one. The first one can stay with the philosophers.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">I keep coming back to the Sacks cases when I think about scope. The patient who can't recognise faces still has most of a person intact. The model of him that matters for a product decision doesn't need to include his face recognition at all. The machinery is separable. That's permission to build small pieces and test them.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">I don't know if this goes anywhere. I have a tendency to pick up threads and not finish them, and this could be another one. But the question has a shape now, and shapes are easier to come back to.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" id="open-questions" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">Unanswered</h2>
      <ul data-astro-cid="s">
        <li data-astro-cid="s">Where is the line between a model of a person and a claim about personhood? If the model gets better, does the line move, or was the line always in the wrong place?</li>
        <li data-astro-cid="s">If realism means including the bugs, what's the right ratio of correct behaviour to broken behaviour?</li>
        <li data-astro-cid="s">If I simulate a thousand people, am I simulating a thousand selves or one machine with a thousand parameter sets? Is there a difference that matters?</li>
        <li data-astro-cid="s">Does spending a lot of time thinking about people as functions change how I treat actual people? In which direction?</li>
        <li data-astro-cid="s">The books said the self is narrative, assembled from parts. If that's true, what exactly am I modelling when I model "a person"? The narrative? The parts? The assembly process?</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <aside class="study-backlink" data-astro-cid="s">
      <p class="study-backlink-label" data-astro-cid="s">Written before any of the work this question led to. Noting the starting position while it's still the starting position.</p>
    </aside>
  </article>

  <section id="related" data-astro-cid="s">
    <h2 data-astro-cid="s">Related Studies</h2>
    <div class="study-related-grid" data-astro-cid="s">
      {related.map(r => {
        const rd = new Date(r.date).toLocaleDateString("en-US", { month: "long", year: "numeric" });
        return (
          <a class="study-related-card" href={`/studies/${r.slug}`} data-astro-cid="s">
            <p class="study-related-date" data-astro-cid="s">{rd}</p>
            <h3 class="study-related-title" data-astro-cid="s">{r.title}</h3>
            <p class="study-related-abstract" data-astro-cid="s">{r.abstract}</p>
            <span class="study-related-link" data-astro-cid="s">Read &rarr;</span>
          </a>
        );
      })}
    </div>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
  .study-article[data-astro-cid="s"] { max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto; padding-block: 32px 48px; }
  .study-header[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-bottom: 40px; }
  .study-date[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .study-title[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.2rem); font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.15; letter-spacing: -0.02em; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .study-abstract[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--muted); max-width: 58ch; margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .study-keywords[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; }
  .study-kw[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: 0.03em; color: var(--green); background: var(--chip-bg); border: 1px solid var(--hairline-soft); border-radius: 5px; padding: 2px 8px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-top: 36px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] h2[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.01em; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] p[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] p[data-astro-cid="s"] strong[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-weight: 500; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] em[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-style: italic; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] ul[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 16px; padding-left: 1.4rem; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] li[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-bottom: 10px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] pre[data-astro-cid="s"] { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px 20px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 18px 0 22px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] pre[data-astro-cid="s"] code[data-astro-cid="s"] { background: none; padding: 0; border-radius: 0; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--ink); }
  .study-backlink[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: 10px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 20px 22px; margin-top: 40px; }
  .study-backlink-label[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; color: var(--ink); margin: 0; }
  #related[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-top: 44px; }
  .study-related-grid[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: grid; gap: 10px; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .study-related-card[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: flex; flex-direction: column; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 18px; transition: border-color 0.15s, transform 0.15s; }
  .study-related-card[data-astro-cid="s"]:hover { border-color: var(--green); transform: translateY(-1px); }
  .study-related-date[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .study-related-title[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.3; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .study-related-abstract[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .study-related-link[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--green); margin-top: auto; }
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .study-article[data-astro-cid="s"] { padding-block: 24px 36px; padding-inline: 8px; }
    .study-backlink[data-astro-cid="s"] { flex-direction: column; }
  }
</style>
